THOUGHT: The error is about assigning a @Nullable expression to a @NonNull field. Here, `elements` is inherited from `SparseIntSet` and is treated as possibly null in this subclass (e.g., `if (elements != null)` and `elements = null` in `remove`). In `intersectWith`, `cr` is declared `int[] cr = null;` and later assigned to `elements` via `elements = cr;`, which violates NullAway’s non-null contract even though logic ensures `cr` is non-null when used. The simplest fix is to avoid assigning a nullable variable to `elements` by introducing a non-null local `result` that is only assigned when `cr` is allocated, and then assign `elements = result;`. I also need to ensure that when `ci == 0` (empty intersection), we assign a non-null empty array instead of null to keep NullAway happy. After editing, I’ll run the Gradle compile to confirm the NullAway error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/wala-util && sed -i '252,282s/int\[\] cr = null; // allocate on demand/int[] cr = null; // allocate on demand
    int[] result = elements; // will hold the new non-null backing array/' src/main/java/com/ibm/wala/util/intset/MutableSparseIntSet.java && sed -i '270,282c\        if (cr == null) {
          cr = new int[al]; // allocate enough (i.e. too much)
        }
        cr[ci++] = ar[ai];
        ai++;
        bi++;
      }
    }

    // now compact cr to ''just enough''
    size = ci;
    if (ci == 0) {
      result = new int[0];
    } else if (cr != null && ci != cr.length) {
      result = new int[ci];
      System.arraycopy(cr, 0, result, 0, ci);
    } else if (cr != null) {
      result = cr;
    }
    elements = result;
    return;' src/main/java/com/ibm/wala/util/intset/MutableSparseIntSet.java && ./gradlew compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -E "NullAway: .*errors" -n new_build_output.log || tail -n 80 new_build_output.log```

